    "translation": "I leave my car in the parking lot, appointment confirmation in hand, and make my way through a maze of small buildings marked by minimalist signs: surgery, obstetrics, phlebotomy. Finally, I find what I was looking for: the endoscopy and gastrointestinal surgery department. Its appearance was not very reassuring. The slightly crooked shutters close the windows with a lazy, imprecise motion. The walls are peeling in large, impressionistic patches, and the grass is growing rogue in the flower beds. Hoping that the oft-mentioned cuts to the national healthcare budget haven’t affected the medical equipment, I go inside. Fortunately, the interior is in better condition. I stop in front of the ward door and wait to be called. In front of me, an elderly man, I’d say in his seventies, is waiting, holding a prescription in his hands very similar to mine. I have no reason to strike up a conversation, so I sit and wait. Shortly after, an elderly nurse arrives who hands me a paper form and asks me to sign it. I sign it three times to certify my willingness to undergo the necessary treatment and to release the hospital from any legal liability. Nothing out of the ordinary. Meanwhile, the elderly gentleman is ushered inside. I take mental note of the time to see how long he’ll be in there and therefore estimate how long I’ll be in the doctors’ hands after him, but unexpectedly, my hypothesis is disproven because the man comes out almost immediately. Okay, it’s my turn. The elderly nurse has me undress and put on a gown that’s completely open in the back. Makes sense. She asks me if I need to stop by the restroom and, in fact, even though I haven’t had anything to drink for a couple of hours, I feel the urge to urinate. That makes sense, too. I make a quick pit stop at the bathroom and enter the procedure room."
