    "translation": "Based on a few summaries I found online, ones intentionally not too in-depth, I’ve picked up some basic information about my intestines and the procedure. The entry point will obviously be the anus, even though, strictly speaking, it would be an exit. Once inside, one encounters the first segment, the rectum, which then gives way to the colon proper, which is essentially shaped like two columns and an epistyle. The colon runs up and down. Collectively, the colon frames the small intestine and consists of three segments: the first (on the left) runs upward, the second runs horizontally (just above the navel) from left to right, and the last (on the right) runs downward. Now, what I just said is not entirely true, because it depends, as with everything else in life, on the direction in which you travel through it. Therefore, the description refers to the colonoscope, which must enter the colon and travel through it in the opposite direction of the feces. These long, straight stretches are connected by junctions, known as flexures, which are the most difficult part to navigate, but nothing impossible."
