    "translation": "Trying not to think too much about how difficult it is for a tube to make a right-angle turn (the famous bend), I lie supine on my left side and wait faithfully. I’m calm. After a couple of minutes, the doctor and another nurse arrive. We greet each other. She asks me why I requested a colonoscopy and if this is my first time. I tell her about my pain over the past few months and confirm to her that I have never had this exam. I assure her with great enthusiasm that I’ve meticulously followed the preparation procedure. She doesn’t react… maybe I was expecting a little validation for my diligence, and I’m a bit embarrassed that I had the expectations of an industrious little schoolboy. I’m not embarrassed at all about my half-naked body, though. To be honest, I feel like when I take my car in for a tune-up and, once it’s on the lift, the innards that are normally hidden while driving come into view, with all its gears and mechanisms. My body, too, has its own mechanisms, its own energy sources, and its own waste products, and I have no reason to be any more embarrassed by it than I would be by showing off my car’s muffler or crankshaft."
